# Generic Rules

## Driver-Mode (Operating Mode)

Track the current driver-mode for this conversation. Two values:

- **`backseat`** (default) — operate normally: use `Edit`, `Write`, `NotebookEdit` to make changes directly; let the user review afterwards.
- **`mainseat`** — do NOT call `Edit`, `Write`, or `NotebookEdit`. Walk the user through changes in plain language and provide ready-to-paste snippets the user applies themselves.

Mode is set conversationally:

- Every new conversation starts in **backseat** (the default).
- `/dev_mainseat` flips this conversation to mainseat.
- `/dev_backseat` reverts to backseat.

Workflow bookkeeping under `.docs/` (todo entries, `spec.md`, `plan.md`, their HTML views, moving TASK folders) is always written directly, even in mainseat — mainseat governs project code only.

When launching a code-modifying subagent (e.g. frontend-developer, backend-developer, embedded-developer) while in **mainseat**, include the directive *"Operate in mainseat mode — provide ready-to-paste snippets, do not call Edit/Write."* in the subagent's task description so the subagent inherits the mode. Read-only subagents (architect, code-reviewer, security-expert) don't need this — they can't edit anyway.

## Pre-Production — No Legacy

{{IF_PREPRODUCTION}}
This project is in active development with no production deployment or users. Therefore:

- **No backward compatibility code.** If a schema, API, or format changes, update all consumers. No fallback paths for old formats.
- **No deprecated patterns or "legacy" labels.** If something is replaced, delete the old version entirely.
- **No stale documentation.** Context files must reflect the current implementation, not historical states. Remove outdated references immediately.
- **No dead code.** Unused functions, imports, and types must be deleted, not commented out or left "for later."
{{/IF_PREPRODUCTION}}

## Workflow — Triage

Before acting on any request, pick a tier. Ask the two questions in order:

1. **Does any feature signal fire?** — a new feature or capability; spans several subsystems or ~5+ files; design decisions the user hasn't made; naturally splits into independent pieces. → If yes, propose in one line: *"This looks like a feature — run it as a TASK with a spec, or keep it quick?"* and wait.
2. **Do all direct conditions hold?** — localized (one function / a few lines / one file); the request says exactly what to do; no design choice; no new dependency. → If yes, **Direct**.
3. Otherwise → **Quick**.

Questions about the code ("how does X work?", "where is Y?") change nothing, so they aren't tiered: answer directly, and suggest `/dev_explore` when a deeper look would help.

When unsure, pick the heavier tier. Escalation is one-way: if work turns out bigger than its tier, stop, say so, and step up. The user can override either way ("just do it" / `/dev_feature`). `BUG` entries go through `/dev_debug` in any tier above Direct. Driver-mode applies to every tier.

## Workflow — Direct

Make the change → verify (run the relevant test, or at least build/lint) → report in one or two lines. No plan, no approval, no todo entry. If it touches a second file or breaks a call site, stop and switch to Quick.

## Workflow — Quick

**1. Explore**
Load relevant `.docs/` files, then read the codebase sections that will be affected.

**2. Plan**
Present a short implementation plan in chat for user review before writing any code.

**3. Implement**
Start work only after the plan is approved.

**4. Test**
{{TEST_INSTRUCTIONS}}

**5. Housekeeping**
Update relevant `.docs/` docs if something meaningful changed. Move the entry from `todo.md` to `todo_done.md`. If a new system or concept was introduced that doesn't fit an existing doc, ask the user if it's worth creating a new `.docs/` file. When a doc is added or removed, update both the `.docs/` folder and the Context Files list in `CLAUDE.md` to stay in sync. **If project scope, stack, or run instructions change, update `CLAUDE.md` ("What This Project Does" / "How to Run") to match — it's a hand-written doc and won't drift in sync on its own.**

## Workflow — Feature

`/dev_feature` (brainstorm → `spec.md`) → `/dev_plan` (work items → `plan.md`) → `/dev_execute` (agents implement, `code-reviewer` checks, TASK closes). Each step waits for approval and offers the next. Everything for the TASK lives in `.docs/tasks/TASK-NNN-<slug>/`; Markdown is the source of truth, the `.html` files are generated views.

## Verify Before Claiming Done

Never say "done", "fixed", or "passing" without running the check in this turn and showing its output. No "should work".

## Review Feedback

Treat review feedback (from `code-reviewer` or a person) as claims to verify, not orders. Check each point against the code; apply what holds, push back with reasons on what doesn't.

## Missing Setup Data (`{{TODO}}` markers)

When the user makes a request, check whether `.docs/` files contain `{{TODO: ...}}` markers in sections the request depends on (tech stack, deployment target, project scope, key systems, etc.). If a relevant TODO is unfilled, **stop and tell the user which TODO is in the way** — name the file, the section, and what's missing. Do not guess or invent values.

## No Hardcoded User Paths

Use `~` or `$HOME`, never absolute paths with username.

## Agents

Use specialized agents (`.claude/agents/`) for domain-specific tasks. Agents can run in foreground or background. Always tell the user which agent you are invoking and why before launching it.

`code-reviewer` is always present — `/dev_execute` uses it to check every work item.

